  "query_preview": "..\n\n\nKEY MESSAGES:\n\n-  Colder temperatures, with areas of accumulating snow Friday\n   into Saturday.\n\n-  Snow showers Sunday, bringing minor snow accumulations. \n\n-  Warming trend by the middle of next week. \n\nOur next weather system remains on track to drop southward from \nCanada Friday into Saturday. Model guidance portrays a shortwave \ntrough moving along and east of the divide into north-central \nMontana, with northwest flow taking shape over the Northern \nRockies. This system will bring a favorable flow pattern for snow \nshowers along and east of US-93 corridor in western Montana. These\nareas have the highest probability (60-90 percent) for \naccumulating snow (0.10 inches or more), focused during the Friday\nlate afternoon through Saturday morning timeframe. Those \ntraveling across mountain passes along the Divide, such as Marias,\nHomestake, and Marias passes should prepare for winter driving \nconditions given colder temperatures and periods of accumulating \nsnowfall. The highest snow accumulations will focus across the \nhigher elevations of Glacier Park, the Swan and Mission Mountains,\nand the Bob Marshall Wilderness, where northwest flow will \ntrigger numerous orographically driven snow showers. Finally, \nmodel soundings and timeheights suggest a favorable set up for \nisolated snow bands forming downstream of the Bitterroot Crest \nalong the ID/MT border. These bands will be isolated areas of poor\nvisibility and localized snow accumulations along the US-93 and \nI-90 corridors south and east of Missoula Saturday morning. \n\nAn active pattern will continue Sunday into early next weekend, \nwith colder temperatures holding snow levels near valley floors. \nThe next Pacific trough moves along the Oregon and California \ncoast by Sunday, with southwest flow aloft spreading moisture \ninland across the Northern Rockies. Model guidance suggests snow \nlevels around 2,000 feet Sunday morning, with snow showers leading\nto minor snow accumulations across most elevations and areas \nthrough Sunday morning. Snow levels will climb near 3,000 feet \nalong and south of the I-90 corridor by Sunday afternoon, with \nsnow levels remaining down to valley floors in northwest Montana \nthrough the day. This system does not appear to be a major snow \nproducer, with most locations seeing a trace to 1 inch of snow, \nwith mountain areas along the ID/MT border seeing a couple inches.\n\nA warming trend arrives by the middle of next week as a trough \ndeepens off the West Coast and a ridge of high pressure amplifies \nacross the Northern Rockies. Model disagreement increased by next \nWednesday, highlighted by various solutions within ensemble \ncluster analysis fields. Half of cluster members suggest \nincreasing moisture and precipitation associated with an \natmospheric river pushing on the west coast within southwest flow,\nwith the half of cluster members keeping the Northern Rockies \ndry.",
